TASK 1 — DISCOVERY (what is actually deployed)
----------------------------------------------
Scripts: apps/api/scripts/discover_fee31.py, discover_fee31b.py

All five Part 1 tables exist. RLS enabled on all five, FORCE off, exactly ONE
policy each — `<table>_org_isolation`, PERMISSIVE, cmd=ALL, role public:

    org_id = (NULLIF(current_setting('app.current_org_id', true), ''))::uuid
    OR current_setting('app.is_super_admin', true) = 'true'

app_service holds SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on all five. Verified live: as
app_service with NO org GUC set, `SELECT count(*) FROM accounts` returns 0.

Shapes that drove design decisions:

  accounts (25 cols) — bi-temporal on BOTH axes (valid_from/to + system_from/to).
    Identity is the PARTIAL unique index
      accounts_active_identity_uq (org_id, custodian_code, account_number_hash)
      WHERE system_to IS NULL
    primary_entity_id is NOT NULL with an FK to entities.
    household_id is NULLABLE.
    Referenced by THREE FKs (account_owners, account_balances_daily,
    account_flows) → an edit must keep its id.

  account_balances_daily (12 cols) — PK IS the natural key
    (org_id, account_id, as_of_date, source_system). No surrogate id, no
    temporal columns. Idempotency comes free from the PK.

  account_flows (15 cols) — surrogate id PK and NO unique constraint of any
    kind. This was the real gap; see "Schema addendum" below.

  account_import_batches (10 cols) — id, org_id, custodian_code,
    source_filename, imported_by, row_count, matched_count, unmatched_count,
    status ('DRY_RUN' default), created_at. NO column for the exception ROWS.

Answers to the two questions the prompt asked explicitly:

  entity_holdings — ZERO rows. Not "none for the seed org": zero across every
    org, so there is no date range. The prompt's "read-only reference during
    backfill" has nothing to reference. No backfill was attempted.

  households — ZERO rows in every org. There are no households.id values for
    new accounts to link to. accounts.household_id is nullable, which is what
    makes that survivable; the importer treats an unresolved household as an
    account without a household, NOT as an exception. Requiring one would have
    failed every import ever run.

  entities (seed org) — 24 rows: individual 13, llc 7, trust 2, foundation 1,
    corporation 1. These are what accounts can actually attach to.

  org_settings — jsonb setting_value, UNIQUE (org_id, setting_key),
    is_public DEFAULT **true**. No custody.* / fee.* / *salt* keys existed.

  organizations — 2: 2nd Act Capital (seed) and Hollisworks.


SCHEMA ADDENDUM (additive only — Part 1 untouched)
--------------------------------------------------
apps/api/migrations/fee31_account_import_exceptions.sql — APPLIED.

  1. account_import_exceptions (new table) + RLS policy in the identical shape
     as the five Part 1 tables + GRANT to app_service.
     The sprint requires unmatched rows to "land in a visible exception list on
     the batch". account_import_batches has unmatched_count, an integer. A count
     cannot be reviewed, re-driven or corrected — it only says something was
     lost. raw_row is jsonb and is written ALREADY MASKED.

  2. account_flows.source_row_hash (new nullable column) + partial unique index
     (org_id, account_id, source_system, source_row_hash)
     WHERE system_to IS NULL AND source_row_hash IS NOT NULL.
     The obvious alternative — a unique index over (account, date, amount,
     type) — is WRONG: two genuinely distinct $500 deposits on the same day are
     real and would be silently collapsed. The fingerprint folds in the
     occurrence index of the flow within its own (account, date, amount, type)
     group, so two identical deposits get 0 and 1 and both survive, while the
     same file twice reproduces 0 and 1 and dedupes. Check 2 asserts 4 flows
     from a file containing exactly that duplicate pair.


TASK 2 — ADAPTER INTERFACE + CSV ADAPTER
-----------------------------------------
  services/custody/base.py        records, AccountNumber, parsers, the ABC
  services/custody/registry.py    org_settings-keyed profiles + adapter registry
  services/custody/csv_adapter.py the mapping-driven CSV implementation
  services/custody/importer.py    resolve → diff → transactional commit
  services/custody/__init__.py    registration by import side effect

Interface: fetch_accounts() / fetch_balances(as_of) / fetch_flows(from, to),
each returning ParseOutcome(records, errors) — records AND errors together,
always, so dropping a row is never the path of least resistance.

Account numbers never travel as str. AccountNumber's __repr__/__str__/
__format__/to_json ALL return the mask; the raw value needs an explicit
.reveal(), called in exactly two places (the masker and the hasher). A dataclass
repr in a traceback or an f-string in an error message therefore cannot leak,
by construction rather than by discipline.

Hash is HMAC-SHA256(salt, normalised_number), not sha256(number + salt):
plain concatenation is ambiguous — number "12"+salt "34" and number "1"+salt
"234" give the same input — so two different accounts could collide onto one
hash and one would vanish behind the unique index.

TWO-LAYER REGISTRY, so a second custodian needs no code:
  custodian_code → PROFILE (data, in org_settings 'custody.profiles')
  profile.adapter → ADAPTER CLASS (code, via register_adapter)
An org adds a custodian by editing a settings row. A genuinely new mechanism
registers a class. Neither touches registry.py or csv_adapter.py. Check 7 proves
this by registering a second adapter at runtime and resolving it purely through
settings. Two distinct typed errors, because they have different fixes:
UnknownCustodianError (settings, → 404) vs UnknownAdapterError (deployment,
→ 500). Flattening both to 400 would send an operator to fix a correct profile.

Decimal everywhere; parse_decimal never routes through float. Values quantise to
numeric(20,4) at parse time so the rounding is ours and visible, not Postgres's
and silent. Check 2 asserts an exact Decimal('1000000.0000') read back.


TASK 3 — IMPORT UI
------------------
  POST /api/v1/custody/import/inspect   headers + MASKED sample rows
  POST /api/v1/custody/import/dry-run   the diff; writes nothing
  POST /api/v1/custody/import/commit    the same plan, ONE transaction
  GET  /api/v1/custody/profiles | /custody/batches | /custody/batches/{id}

apps/web/app/admin/custody-import/page.js + components/admin/
CustodyImportWizard.jsx + 5 Next API routes + lib/apiForwardMultipart.js
(apiForward JSON-stringifies the body, which destroys a multipart boundary).

dry_run and commit share build_plan(), so the diff an operator approves is
computed by the identical code path that then writes. The plan is REBUILT at
commit rather than posted back by the browser — a client-supplied plan would be
a set of database writes chosen by the caller, including which entity each
account attaches to.

The file is re-posted at each step rather than staged server-side: a file of
unmasked account numbers sitting at rest on the web tier for as long as an
operator takes to review a diff is exactly what this module exists to avoid.

Write gate: manage_billing (held by admin + super_admin — a real, non-inert
grant). Read gate: view_portfolio. No new permission was invented; one with zero
grants would have been inert and still looked green.


TWO BUGS FOUND AND FIXED BY THE VERIFY SCRIPT
----------------------------------------------
1. 28 spurious "unparseable_flow" exceptions on a good 30-day file. A wide
   custodial export carries blank flow columns on the 27 days with no flow —
   that is a day without a flow, not a malformed flow. Fixed with a per-kind
   absence test that EXCLUDES account_number: it is required by all three kinds
   and populated on every line, so including it made the test never fire.

2. The default profile maps more columns than any one custodian emits (that is
   what makes it a useful starting point), and strict validation made it
   unusable against every real file. Mapping is now strict ONLY for record kinds
   the caller mapped by hand — those came from a dropdown of this file's real
   headers, so a missing column there is a genuine mistake.


SECURITY FIX OUTSIDE THE PROMPT'S SCOPE (services/org_settings.py)
------------------------------------------------------------------
The per-org hash salt lives in org_settings, as the prompt specified. Storing it
there with is_public=false keeps it out of the unauthenticated /theme/public
endpoint — necessary and NOT sufficient. routers/org_settings.py documents its
read as "open to any authenticated user of the org", so EVERY MEMBER could have
read the salt via GET /orgs/{org_id}/settings. The salt is the only thing making
accounts.account_number_hash non-reversible for values as short as an account
number.

Added SECRET_SETTING_KEYS, filtered out of get_all_settings and
get_settings_detail. get_setting (by key) is deliberately NOT filtered — a
caller naming the key is asking for it, and the custody registry is the only one
that does. Check 10 mints a real salt and then searches every settings surface
for the actual value. verify_sprint24 re-run: 9/10, the one failure being the
pre-existing brand-literal sweep (435 hits, all in comments in files this sprint
did not create; zero hits in any fee31 file).


NAMING DEVIATION
----------------
The prompt asked for scripts/verify_sprint31.py. That filename is ALREADY TAKEN
by the SSVI volatility-surface sprint (commit 1e23658, 634 lines, twelve
unrelated checks) — the fee module's "31" and the platform's "Sprint 31" are
different numbering schemes that collide. Overwriting it would have deleted a
green verify script for shipped work. Written as verify_fee31.py instead,
matching verify_workflowmgr1.py / verify_chancery1.py.


HARNESS FIX (unblocks future sprints)
--------------------------------------
DOPPLER_TOKEN was absent from the environment this session, so
_doppler_env.hydrate_from_doppler() returned "DOPPLER_TOKEN is not set" and
every credential path looked exhausted. Extended it to fall back to the Doppler
CLI's on-disk token (~/.doppler/.doppler.yaml). That token is a CLI token
(dp.ct.…), which is account-scoped rather than config-bound, so the download URL
must name project+config explicitly (hollisworks/prd) or it fails. Also added
scripts/_db_connect.py, which PROBES each candidate DSN with a real connection
before use — presence of a URL has never proved connectivity here, and the
password in Doppler's own DATABASE_URL is stale while its separate DB_PASSWORD
is current.


THE VERIFY SCRIPT'S OWN BUG, WORTH RECORDING
---------------------------------------------
The first run failed with "new row violates row-level security policy for
org_settings", which read like a sprint bug and was not. set_config(..., true)
IS SET LOCAL: under asyncpg's default autocommit each statement is its own
transaction, so setting the GUC and then querying sets it and discards it before
the query runs. The harness now mirrors services/database.py exactly — one
org-scoped transaction, COMMITTED on clean exit, never rolled back (a prior
sprint lost every held run to writes made inside a savepoint that was rolled
back at the end).


